    {
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-regular-conditional-laws-C011",
      "verdict": "partial",
      "gap": "Durrett presents this computation as Example 4.1.8, Borel's paradox (p. 210): at first glance both the longitude theta and the great-circle angle phi should be uniform; theta is uniform but phi is not; the paradox evaporates because phi is independent of theta in either parametrization, so the conditional law is the unconditional one, nonuniform because there is more land near the equator than near the North Pole. The handbook (body 222-248, annex 346-389) proves the independence and the |cos psi|/4 density, but never names Borel's paradox, states the naive uniform expectation, or gives the source's equator explanation; the only route mention of the Borel paradox (bayes-factor-computation line 1041) is unrelated and unlinked.",
      "blocker": null,
      "evidence": [
