    {
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-regular-conditional-laws-C012",
      "verdict": "partial",
      "gap": "The source's existence caveat (p. 214) is not fully preserved: regular conditional distributions do not always exist, the first example being due to Dieudonne (1948) (see Doob 1953, p. 624, or Faden 1985), and the stated reason that although P(X in union A_n | G) = sum_n P(X in A_n | G) a.s. for disjoint A_n, the exceptional sets may pile up when S contains enough countable disjoint families. The boundary (body 250-257) only says existence is not asserted beyond standard Borel targets, and def:p15-w02b-p15-008-limits item 3 records the bare non-existence statement; the Dieudonne attribution, the references and the pile-up explanation appear nowhere on the route.",
